A lot more falls out of it.

A contract is usually built to survive mistrust. A covenant is built to preserve fidelity.

That creates several useful oppositions:

* Contract asks: What am I owed?
    Covenant asks: What am I responsible for preserving?
* Contract minimizes ambiguity.
    Covenant must survive ambiguity. You cannot prewrite every future circumstance, so it carries a governing spirit/invariant instead.
* Contract breach activates remedies.
    Covenant breach activates repair, reconciliation, judgment, forgiveness, or ultimately severance. The object is not merely damages; it is the relationship.
* Contracts terminate when performance completes.
    Covenants can outlive the original performance, participants, or even generation. They create continuity through time.
* Contracts are generally symmetrical transactions.
    Covenants can be radically asymmetrical. Parent/child is an obvious example: responsibility does not disappear because the child cannot reciprocate equivalently.

And there is a particularly interesting computational distinction:

A contract stores instructions.
A covenant stores a generator.

The contract tries to enumerate:
IF circumstance A → action B.

A covenant gives you something closer to:
In any circumstance, preserve invariant C.

Then the participants have to compile the correct action locally.

That means covenants are extraordinarily compression-efficient. Instead of encoding a million future cases, you transmit an identity, loyalty, principle, or bond capable of regenerating appropriate conduct as circumstances change.

That connects directly to another distinction:

law of the letter ↔ law of the spirit.

The letter is specification-heavy.
The spirit is reconstruction-heavy.

Neither is automatically superior—the danger of pure “spirit” is that people can rationalize anything. So the strongest architecture might actually be:

Covenant = invariant
Contract = interface/specification
Law = boundary
Judgment = interpretation
Grace = recovery mechanism

Then another one:

Promises concern actions.
Oaths bind the speaker.
Contracts bind parties to terms.
Covenants bind identities across time.

And perhaps the sharpest formulation:

A contract tells you what happens if the relationship fails. A covenant tells you why the relationship must survive.

That is probably why marriage, military allegiance, constitutional orders, religious traditions, family bonds, and your Pact metaphor naturally drift toward covenant-language. Their defining problem is not merely exchange. It is continuity under unforeseen conditions.

Which gives you a very Pantheon-looking primitive:

Covenant = continuity without exhaustive foresight.